      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Current social capital theory exhibits a significant evolution, moving beyond initial definitions centered solely on bonding and bridging ties. Early contributions from thinkers like Putnam and Bourdieu established frameworks for analyzing the resources embedded within social networks, differentiating between various forms of capital. Contemporary scholarship, incorporating insights from Granovetter and Burt, heavily emphasizes the structural role of weak ties and network positions in resource mobilization and economic outcomes. Furthermore, the field has increasingly integrated agency perspectives, seen in the work of Portes and Lin, while also examining governance structures through scholars like Ostrom.",
